{"name":"Yellow Coneflower","hex":"#edb856","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yellow coneflower","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several flowering plants:\nRudbeckia hirta\nRudbeckia pinnata\nEchinacea paradoxa","Rudbeckia hirta"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow_coneflower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yellow coneflower","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow coneflower is a common name for several plants and may refer to:"},{"items":["Echinacea paradoxa, native to southern Missouri, Arkansas, and south-central Oklahoma in the United States","Rudbeckia hirta, native to eastern and central North America","Ratibida pinnata, native to the central and eastern United States and Ontario"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow_coneflower","wikidata":"Q126974658"},{"title":"Coneflower","blocks":[{"text":"Coneflower is a common name of several genera of flowering plants:"},{"items":["Dracopis","Echinacea","Rudbeckia","Ratibida"]},{"items":["Isopogon"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coneflower","wikidata":"Q5159477"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_sour